# Internal records · `guide:acts:records`

**Section (Acts)** · `sec:acts:area-acts` {{ row('org.peer-network.testing.claim-areas', owner='RECORDS', area='acts').stake }} The first slice implements local validation under (`[ROOT-sig:design:records]`) and (`[ROOT-model:design:accepted-act]`); it does not certify authentication, formation, membership, family endpoint typing or a standing result.

**Definition (Structural identity)** · `def:acts:identity` An ActId consists of its exact public author identifier, author-local sequence and exact family name. An EdgeId consists of its parent ActId and Binary, A or T role. Authoritative position, finalization epoch and removal facts are not identifier fields. Equal endpoints do not collapse distinct edge identities.

**Definition (References)** · `def:acts:references` PublicReference is an act, edge or exact namespace/name identity. ValidatedHistory derives act and leg references from complete locally validated history acts; separately supplied external names cannot inject an act or edge. A batch identity already in history refuses as a duplicate. resolve_public_reference takes ValidatedRecords and distinguishes History from Batch, refusing missing or malformed queries. Tombstones never erase these identities. No database or codec is consulted.

**Definition (Projection ownership)** · `def:acts:projection` A Binary layout owns one Binary structural leg; a Paired layout owns A and T together. Neither leg owns a removal state: any candidate leg removal field refuses. RemovableProjections carries the act's independent content and dependency facts; a tombstone must follow the act's authoritative position. Dependency edge status derives from this act-owned dependency fact, never from a co-leg. Both structural legs and immutable protocol identities survive a dependency tombstone, following (`[ROOT-amend:design:act-removal-facts]`). This snapshot validator admits no mutation or resurrection operation.

**Definition (Structural body)** · `def:acts:body` The first local body representation declares its projection layout and named exact integer quantities with explicit units and vintages. Validation preserves these fields without conversion and refuses empty or duplicate quantity names and empty units. This bounded integer carrier is not the later family census, rational valuation or external byte profile; those contracts must supply their own types before use. No codec or canonical encoding is assigned here under (`[ROOT-amend:design:bite-one-internal-envelopes]`).

**Definition (Act carrier)** · `def:acts:carrier` CandidateAct is unchecked input: structural identifier, body, candidate legs, act-owned projections, immutable protocol references, public authentication evidence, finalization epoch and authoritative position. ActId alone contains author and family. ValidatedAct, ValidatedLeg, BoundaryInputs and PrincipalClosure have private fields and read-only accessors, so only validation and assembly construct them. ValidatedAct is a distinct owned view with ValidatedLeg values that have no candidate removal slot; dependency_status takes ValidatedRecords and derives an edge's status from its owning act, without storing a second status on the leg. ValidatedRecords exposes borrowed views of its private immutable snapshot, never candidates. Nonempty authentication evidence is retained without certifying authenticity.

**Definition (Local validation)** · `def:acts:validation` validate_record checks a complete candidate batch against ValidatedHistory and opaque declarations, then owns a distinct ValidatedRecords snapshot. Duplicate identities, missing references, missing co-legs, invalid local fields and dependencies return typed ValidationError variants. Validation preserves complete acts, quantities, units, vintages and authoritative positions without an encoding. The unpublished error surface remains exhaustive; InvalidCause, DependencyCause and DeclarationCause distinguish local reasons. Every public operation is counted and timed at every level with an explicit refusal outcome for contract rejection; only trace captures full content. The executable owns bootstrap. The development-only test-support feature exposes the shared finite-DAG generator; default production builds have no codec, graph or testkit dependency.


**Definition (Opaque declarations)** · `def:acts:declarations` DeclarationId contains namespace, name and edition; Declaration retains announcement and effective boundaries without a payload. Duplicate identities and simultaneous successors refuse. Empty namespaces, empty names and late announcements have distinct DeclarationCause values; malformed selection queries refuse as InvalidSelectionKey, independently of edition availability. select_effective_declaration selects by effective boundary rather than edition number or input order, retaining historical answers under (`[ROOT-amend:design:bite-one-declaration-selection]`). Bite 2 confirms or explicitly changes the late-announcement rule; declaration contents remain there.

**Definition (Dependency and parent content)** · `def:acts:dependencies` DependencyProjection has FullEmpty, Full and Tombstoned states. Full contains a nonempty, sorted, duplicate-free, self-free list of act identifiers for placement. StructuralBody.asserted_parents is a separate non-removable, sorted, duplicate-free, self-free Vec of ActId; only it defines the causal-parent relation. Both resolve to strictly earlier acts in history or proposals, never leg identifiers. Their derived order is author bytes, then sequence, then family bytes. MAX_DEPENDENCIES and MAX_ASSERTED_PARENTS are provisional 1024-entry bounds; published count parameters and absent identifier byte bounds remain held for bite 2 under (`[ROOT-amend:design:provisional-carriage-bounds]`). This separates the paper’s plain-mentioned ``def:graph:act-causal-parents`` and ``def:graph:act-dependency-projection``; no paper data is transcribed here.

**Definition (Validated history)** · `def:acts:history` validate_history checks previously accepted whole acts and external names before deriving all act and leg references together. It does not certify host admission or cryptographic evidence. validate_record resolves both relation lists against that fixed history and the proposed batch, with strictly earlier target positions. Earlier proposals remain outside history: processing order cannot enlarge it. The owned snapshot admits no isolated history edge or post-validation mutation.

**Definition (Boundary inputs)** · `def:acts:boundary-inputs` assemble_boundary_inputs takes ValidatedRecords and a position cut: history precedes it and proposals are at or after it. It memoizes each proposal’s asserted-parent ancestors outside history once, preserving overlaps and all co-legs inside whole acts. History parents add nothing and tombstoned dependency carriage has no effect. Every closure includes its root; their union is exactly the proposals. Output follows authoritative position with structural identity breaking presentation ties. Work is an optional exact count of node visits, parent inspections, inherited-member insert attempts and emitted members; overflow is unavailable, never zero. The separate records assembly_steps counter mirrors those steps; work_operations remains one per invocation. Pure field accessors inherit their enclosing operation rather than counting another act access. No partition blocks or exhaustion budget are invented under (`[ROOT-amend:design:boundary-snapshot-representation]`).

**Amendment (First slice evidence)** · `amend:acts:first-slice-evidence` The supported premises for (`[ROOT-gate:design:bite-1]`) are: Finite structural, act-removal, whole-history, canonical dependency and asserted-parent, opaque declaration and outside-history causal closure-input fixtures, with local unit/public/integration levels, shared conformance, recorded inner and outer properties and runtime outcomes; no partition, family formation or declaration payload semantics is claimed. Their test-site evidence is (`claim:acts:identity-is-structural`), (`claim:acts:complete-acts-are-counted-once`), (`claim:acts:duplicates-refuse-and-parallel-identities-survive`), (`claim:acts:both-co-legs-are-required`), (`claim:acts:protocol-references-outlive-dependency-removal`), (`claim:acts:invalid-local-fields-refuse`), (`claim:acts:removal-belongs-to-act-projections`), (`claim:acts:references-resolve-against-accepted-history`), (`claim:acts:opaque-editions-preserve-as-of-selection`), (`claim:acts:ambiguous-declaration-histories-refuse`), (`claim:acts:dependency-lists-have-canonical-content`), (`claim:acts:dependencies-require-earlier-accepted-acts`), (`claim:acts:history-owns-its-structural-references`), (`claim:acts:overlapping-closures-retain-complete-inputs`), (`claim:acts:incomplete-boundary-inputs-refuse`), (`claim:acts:asserted-parents-are-canonical-and-earlier`), (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:records-entry-is-observed-without-changing-results]`), (`claim:acts:generated-closures-match-independent-search`), (`claim:acts:single-corruptions-retain-typed-causes`), (`claim:acts:shared-conformance-preserves-whole-acts`), (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:generated-records-refusals-retain-outcomes]`), (`[TESTKIT-claim:testkit:recorded-seed-replays-a-shrunk-failure]`). The complete bite was independently admitted in (`[ROOT-amend:decomposition:bite-one-admission]`); the broader partition and family claims remain in their assigned bites.

**Definition (Shared test support)** · `def:acts:test-support` The test-support feature contains records-dag-1: shrinkable word vectors select layouts, causal parents and placement dependencies independently over two history acts and two to ten proposals, including live and valid tombstoned projection states. The inner public-surface predicates compare closures with a naive transitive search, exact identity sets and work counts, original, reversed and seeded-shuffled presentations, and whole-leg ownership. Nineteen named corruptions cover duplicate identities, missing co-legs, co-leg removal, unresolved references, parent and dependency missing/late/noncanonical/self/over-bound states, history duplicates, isolated history edges and boundary placement. pn-testkit supplies the recorded property configuration and proptest shrinking; pn-verification consumes the same generator through public APIs, with its own fixed-point closure oracle and expected-cause table. The two over-bound mutations also repeat an unresolved identity: they establish count-check precedence over duplicate and missing identities, not isolated invalidity. No independent implementation is claimed.


**Section (Numerics)** · `sec:numerics:area-numerics` {{ row('org.peer-network.testing.claim-areas', owner='RECORDS', area='numerics').stake }} The pure slice follows (`[ROOT-inv:design:units]`) and (`[ROOT-sig:design:numeric-kernel]`), under (`[ROOT-amend:decomposition:numeric-backend]`) and (`[ROOT-amend:decomposition:numeric-representation]`).

**Definition (Exact carriers)** · `def:numerics:exact-carriers` NumericContext owns explicit CAS resource limits and an accuracy comparison-attempt ledger. Literal retains its original text and canonical exact rational; Computed retains exact arithmetic output; RoundedDisplay is nonbinding candidate text with no conversion to a binding wall. Rational arithmetic never reads a display, metric or clock. The current exact arithmetic subset is rational; the symbolic ridge floor is retained by definition and unresolved comparisons are INDECISIVE. ActionCount cannot be passed to apply_cost, and RoundedDisplay cannot be passed to charged_price; compile-fail examples enforce both distinctions. Pure immutable carrier accessors inherit their producing operation, while every numeric boundary carries full public content at trace and counts outcomes at every level.

**Definition (Dimensioned values)** · `def:numerics:dimensioned-values` BurnAmount, BurnPerAction, ActionCount, FullStanding, ReducedStanding and ReducedBurn are distinct types. Reduction retains the named positive Numeraire, including its burn unit and exact per-action value. Recoinage co-scales amounts and numeraire into the new unit; market repricing divides the amount by the positive market factor at fixed purchasing power and leaves the numeraire fixed, so reduced quantities also change inversely. UnitCost is a positive external denomination per named burn unit with public applicable terms, with no universal lower bound; it multiplies BurnAmount only, including ActionCount times the per-action sum theta plus numeraire times the reduced coordinate. RecordingEventCost is separate audit evidence. Width fixtures compare declared family inputs; no fence evaluation or universal family law is implemented here.

**Definition (Shared numeric inputs)** · `def:numerics:shared-inputs` The test-support feature adds records-numerics-1: six fixed word-vector cases and a bounded mapping to nonnegative burn and action quantities and positive numeraire and market factors. Both consumers share these inputs and shrinking; each owns its expectations. The inner tier checks exact values and coordinate equalities; pn-verification uses integer cross-products over the bounded input domain. Each property inherits the environment-reading pn-testkit configuration and the consumer's recorded seed. This is independent checking of one implementation, not a second numeric implementation or unrestricted theorem.

**Section (Declarations)** · `sec:declarations:area-declarations` {{ row('org.peer-network.testing.claim-areas', owner='RECORDS', area='declarations').stake }} This slice supplies payload semantics for (`[ROOT-model:design:declarations]`) and (`[ROOT-model:design:wall-floor]`), without implementing durable history or controller evaluation.

**Definition (Constants edition)** · `def:declarations:constants-edition` ConstantsHistory binds exactly one payload to every validated opaque DeclarationId in the numeric/constants namespace-name family and resolves through select_effective_declaration. Each payload retains the formula and controller editions, chosen floor, numeraire, source constant definitions, permitted override names, domain profiles, comparison procedure, authentication realization and publication profile. Structural floor is excluded from epoch overrides. Floor explicitly chooses exact 3/37 or the defining ridge image p/(1-p); neither is a default. The ridge variant names the defining real of the paper's ``case:epoch:safety-wall-floor-ridge-floor``, never its rounded root or enclosure endpoints. Exact rational comparison admits equality and greater walls, refuses lesser walls and leaves unresolved or exhausted comparison INDECISIVE; that outcome cannot create a charged price. Symbolic source definitions are retained as source, not certified by evaluating their spelling. Profile and controller identities remain opaque pending their owning bites.

**Section (Ledger vintages)** · `sec:ledger:area-ledger` {{ row('org.peer-network.testing.claim-areas', owner='RECORDS', area='ledger').stake }} The carrier realizes the scoped coordinate requirement of (`[ROOT-inv:design:charged-value]`); accepted burn increments and ledger accounting remain in their later slices.

**Definition (Charged coordinate)** · `def:ledger:charged-coordinate` ChargedPrice privately retains one exact rational wall, its numeraire and their derived burn-per-action price after checking the declared floor. A caller cannot independently set a second price. ChargedVintage retains that pair, action count, boundary and exact charged amount; later price creation has no reference that can mutate it. This is the consummated carrier witness, not a host admission or conserved-ledger implementation.

**Amendment (Pure numeric evidence)** · `amend:numerics:pure-slice-evidence` The rational-carrier premises of (`[ROOT-gate:design:bite-2]`) are exercised by (`claim:numerics:recoinage-and-repricing-have-distinct-laws`), (`claim:numerics:external-cost-projects-burn-units-only`), (`claim:numerics:boundary-comparisons-remain-sound-or-indecisive`), (`claim:numerics:width-verdicts-require-the-family-law`), (`claim:numerics:literal-signs-and-resources-refuse-explicitly`), (`claim:declarations:effective-editions-preserve-historical-constants`), (`claim:declarations:floor-sources-match-declared-constants`), (`claim:ledger:later-prices-preserve-charged-vintages`), (`claim:numerics:generated-numeric-coordinates-retain-their-laws`), (`claim:numerics:shared-numeric-cases-preserve-exact-contracts`) and (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:numeric-carriers-are-observed-without-changing-values]`). The bite gate remains held for real PostgreSQL history and secret integration (host storage implementer after provisioning), and real signed Elements/test-asset integration (wallet and development-network implementers after provisioning). Exact ridge refinement and fence evaluation remain with the numerical kernel in bite 6; declared count and identifier byte bounds remain with declaration-policy admission rather than being invented by the rational representation limits.

**Section (Burn evidence)** · `sec:burn:area-burn` {{ row('org.peer-network.testing.claim-areas', owner='RECORDS', area='burn').stake }} The pure carrier checks implement the local premises of (`[ROOT-model:design:burn]`) and (`[ROOT-rule:design:marker-recognition]`). They do not establish external destruction, finality or a derived burn snapshot.

**Definition (Burn carriers)** · `def:burn:carriers` BurnInput retains the exact sampled amount for every declared read address in an opaque address space, effective edition, sample convention and boundary. ValidatedBurn checks complete publication and unit agreement, without recomputing the sampled map. Addresses are names, not identities. BurnAmount is nonnegative by construction, and UnitCost retains its positive dimensioned projection without a universal lower bound. Snapshot and increment derivation remain with bite 5.

**Definition (Required burn disclosures)** · `def:burn:disclosures` BurnEvidence is a companion audit record, bound to the carrier's effective edition and boundary sample convention; a mismatch refuses. Required publications include bootstrap capacity with its interval explanation, unit-cost projection, settlement depth, finality assumptions, provenance, separately declared recording-event costs, and either an operator bound with its proof or an explanation of no accruing operator role. Missing or blank evidence is a typed publication refusal even if no formula reads it. An empty pre-opening interval requires a published exact zero and explanation; it does not cap later burns. An explicitly empty recording-cost list means no such charges. Proof and finality content is preserved, not mathematically or externally certified by string presence.

**Definition (Fixed marker policy)** · `def:burn:marker-policy` bind_marker_policy is the only public constructor of ValidatedPolicy. Genesis comes from the single deployment/genesis structural declaration, whose announcement and effective boundary must not follow genesis; the policy field is only an asserted copy and must agree. It resolves the fixed marker/policy declaration family at that genesis through select_effective_declaration, requires the policy identity to equal that family's name, its edition to equal the selected edition in canonical decimal spelling, its publication to equal the announcement, and its complete content digest to equal the selected declaration’s MarkerPolicy payload. The digest uses SHA-256 over a versioned domain, fixed field order, big-endian u64 string lengths and integers, UTF-8 strings, and explicit boolean and enum tags; this is internal commitment framing, not a protocol document encoding. Missing or ambiguous deployment declarations, moved genesis, missing digests, substituted payloads, and absent or late declarations and mismatched payloads refuse with typed causes. ValidatedPolicy freezes policy identity, edition and sequence, publication before genesis, an open placement procedure, cadence, verifier profile, accepted-set encoding and candidate-selection rule. FirstValidInHistoryOrder is admitted for open placement: it tries candidates by their unique disclosed history positions and retains the first that passes. UniquePerIndex is admitted only for realizations that establish structural admission of at most one candidate per index, so no party can place a competitor; its refusal of competing candidates reports them in disclosed position order and exposes a violated premise rather than enforcing open-placement liveness. Vector presentation is not history order. Duplicate positions refuse validation. A predecessor designates the unique disclosed history position of the selected candidate at the immediately preceding index. Names are reusable lookup labels: a later copied name cannot displace or stall FirstValidInHistoryOrder. UniquePerIndex still stops for any competing positions at one index because that violates its deployment assumption, independently of names. The shared fixtures declare UniquePerIndex; separate reversal and shuffle witnesses exercise both rules. The epoch-read stability buffer must strictly exceed the published settlement depth. CompleteList, PublicCommitment and WholePrefixAcceptAll are separate choices; a record order never substitutes for acceptance under another choice. Later declarations cannot mutate this retained policy.

**Definition (One-history lineage)** · `def:burn:lineage` HistoryInput names one history and its complete disclosed burn records and marker candidates. Marker structural equality includes its external lookup name, history, policy identity and edition, sequence, index, unique disclosed history position, predecessor position, lineage and interpretation data. Names are lookup labels and may recur; only the predecessor position selects a candidate. A history position is the realization’s inclusion coordinate in the history being read. A successor must follow its predecessor’s position; successors of a predecessor re-included at another position must be placed again, because alternative histories are read afresh. Recognition requires the policy's initial name at zero, no predecessor, empty acceptance and an explicitly empty snapshot; recognized subsequent indices and predecessors are immediate; the declared selection rule chooses at most one candidate at each index. Every lineage record must be present and predecessor lineage must be retained. A candidate missing a member or predecessor and successors depending on it remain unrecognized, while the valid preceding prefix survives. The realization's external causal-dependence guarantee remains a host/chain obligation.

**Definition (Complete accepted sets)** · `def:burn:accepted-set` Each fixed marker interpretation contains a complete list, one binding public opening of a complete committed set, or the explicit whole-prefix token under its matching policy. No opening, multiple openings, failed binding verification or a mismatched encoding refuses. Recognition requires predecessor acceptance to be contained in current acceptance and current acceptance to be contained in lineage, within one history. Delayed acceptance is allowed; unaccepted lineage records need not be valid burns. No clipping, enlargement or omission repairs a candidate.

**Definition (Public verification boundary)** · `def:burn:verification-boundary` PublicVerifier is an explicit trusted realization boundary selected by the fixed policy's profile. Its deterministic public checks must verify destruction, exact nonnegative amount and original attribution, and uniquely binding complete-set openings. validate_marker_history retains the public record and proof bytes plus verification outcomes; recognize_markers then consumes only that immutable validated carrier. A malicious or unsound verifier cannot be made sound by these local checks. The fixture verifier binds small public fields and complete sets without cryptography; real Elements evidence and any cryptographic commitment verifier remain held for their adapter and independent review. The verifier must bind destruction to the destroyed object's unique canonical identity under its profile: valid aliases cannot give that object different destruction keys. M4 deduplication relies on this realization duty, which a fixture cannot certify.

**Definition (Pure recognition)** · `def:burn:recognition` recognize_markers checks M1 to M3 of network-v0.29.0, section 02, and the alias-consistency/deduplication prerequisite of M4. It returns RecognizedMarkers containing the recognized prefix and the first rejected candidate with its typed cause (or no candidate for an empty history). FirstValidInHistoryOrder may extend that prefix through a valid competitor while retaining the earlier refusal; it does not repair the rejected candidate. Global publication, verifier-profile and record-identity errors remain validation refusals before recognition. Callers select their boundary from the returned prefix. Recognition is not finality: the host must establish finality at the published settlement depth in bite 8. RecognitionBasis retains history identity, policy identity and edition, verifier profile, burn publication edition and sample convention. Persistence follows (`[ROOT-amend:storage:unsettled-recognition]`): store recognitions only as unsettled SCD2 rows keyed by that basis, with immutable payloads and appended supersession; store sampled amounts only as their publication, never as a settled burn map. A retained refusal is not a terminal boundary when a later valid competitor extends the prefix. Its immutable recognized marker views come with complete accepted record sets and one representative per destruction, preserving its amount and address. Different valid aliases cannot change either. It never sums amounts or derives first-accepted increments. Alternative histories are recognized separately, without imposing acceptance containment across them. All refusals are exhaustive typed BurnError causes while the package is unpublished; a recognition carrying a candidate refusal explicitly records refusal rather than error, including when a valid competitor extends its prefix.

**Definition (Burn fixture support)** · `def:burn:test-support` records-burn-1 maps shrinkable words to bounded amounts and opaque addresses in a valid three-marker history with delayed acceptance and a consistent alias. Thirty-nine controlled corruptions begin from that valid input. The inner consumer owns a cause and prefix table; the outer derives causes and the first affected index from the changed premise, then derives retained names, accepted identities and destruction keys from the base input. Both assert the retained prefix for every recognition refusal and no prefix for validation-level refusals in original and reversed presentations; pn-testkit supplies the inherited property configuration and shrinking, with consumer-qualified outer seeds. These are observations of one implementation under a deliberately noncryptographic public-proof fixture, not independent chain evidence.

**Amendment (Scoped burn recognition evidence)** · `amend:burn:pure-recognition-evidence` The pure premises of (`[ROOT-gate:design:bite-2]`) are exercised by (`claim:burn:missing-required-burn-disclosures-prevent-readiness`), (`claim:burn:markers-fix-initial-state-and-succession`), (`claim:burn:public-openings-recover-complete-policy-sets`), (`claim:burn:recognition-requires-one-complete-history`), (`claim:burn:destruction-aliases-retain-one-contribution-identity`), (`claim:burn:shared-marker-cases-preserve-recognition-contracts`), (`claim:burn:generated-marker-corruptions-retain-typed-causes`) and (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:burn-recognition-retains-observed-outcomes]`). Real destruction/opening verification and causal settlement remain with the wallet and host adapters, storage with the host implementer, and snapshot/first-accepted increments with pn-epoch in bite 5. These wider claims remain held under (`[ROOT-open:design:claim-evidence]`).

**Amendment (Typed per-action numeric join)** · `amend:numerics:per-action-join` Under (`[ROOT-inv:design:units]`), Numeraire retains a positive BurnPerAction, not a BurnAmount. per_action computes a reduced coordinate times that numeraire and is also the sole price image used by charged_price. door_amount takes ActionCount and the per-action sum, producing the BurnAmount which apply_cost accepts; the ambiguous combined_cost operation is removed. ReducedBurn retains its numeraire and action-credit value. recoin_standing and reprice_standing preserve standing's type, so the recoinage and fixed-numeraire repricing witnesses need no text conversion. (`claim:numerics:recoinage-and-repricing-have-distinct-laws`) and (`claim:numerics:external-cost-projects-burn-units-only`) establish these carrier premises; no admission or ledger computation is claimed.

**Amendment (Constants family scope)** · `amend:declarations:family-binding` Under (`[ROOT-amend:design:declaration-family-binding]`), bind_constants binds only numeric/constants editions, leaving every other namespace-name pair to its own payload type. (`claim:declarations:payloads-bind-only-their-declaration-family`) checks mixed histories, missing numeric payloads, foreign payload injection and wrong-family resolution; historical selection retains (`claim:declarations:effective-editions-preserve-historical-constants`).
